## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer mechanisms, wakeup latency, and concrete data structures suitable for embedded or real-time operating systems.

## Key Concepts
- Timers with absolute expiry and optional periodicity
- Heap-based scheduling for efficient expiry checks
- Interrupt-safe wakeups and drift prevention
- Latency testing with mixed workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
function timer_add(heap, expiry, period, task) {
  push(heap, { expiry, period, task });
  siftUp(heap, heap.length - 1);
}

function timer_interrupt(now_ms) {
  while (heap.length > 0 && heap[0].expiry <= now_ms) {
    const timer = popMin(heap);
    timer.task();
    if (timer.period > 0) {
      timer.expiry = now_ms + timer.period;
      push(heap, timer);
    }
  }
}
```

## Testing Considerations
Run mixed CPU-bound and timer-heavy workloads simultaneously. Measure wake latency (actual vs. expected wake time) and jitter (variance in wake intervals) to validate drift prevention and real-time guarantees.

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests the candidate's ability to design a scalable, power-efficient timer subsystem that can handle high-frequency timer events across multiple CPUs while maintaining sub-millisecond wakeup latencies.

## Key Concepts
- **Tickless operation**: Disabling periodic timer ticks during idle periods to reduce power consumption
- **Per-CPU timer buckets**: Local deadline queues that minimize cross-CPU synchronization overhead
- **Global min-heap**: Centralized data structure for tracking the nearest timer expiration across all CPUs
- **Memory ordering**: Using memory barriers to ensure proper visibility of timer state between CPUs
- **Atomic operations**: CAS for lock-free timer insertion and deletion
- **Latency bounds**: Worst-case wakeup latency is determined by hardware interrupt latency plus data structure traversal time

## Code Example
